---
import LegalPage from "../components/LegalPage.astro";
import { GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL } from "../lib/site";

const sections = [
  ["reporting", "Reporting a vulnerability"],
  ["safe-harbor", "Good-faith research"],
  ["model", "What Kassiber protects"],
  ["gaps", "What it does not protect"],
  ["responsibilities", "Your part"],
  ["contact", "Contact"],
] as const;

const SECURITY_MD_URL = `${GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`;
---

<LegalPage
  title="Security Policy · Kassiber"
  description="How to report a vulnerability in Kassiber, what the software protects, and what it deliberately does not."
  heading="Security Policy"
  lede="Kassiber holds your financial history, so it is worth being precise about what it defends against and what it does not. Report vulnerabilities privately; the full threat model lives in SECURITY.md next to the code."
  effectiveDate="2026-07-29"
  lastUpdated="2026-07-29"
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="reporting">
    <p class="section-number">01</p>
    <h2>Reporting a Vulnerability</h2>
    <p class="legal-notice">
      Do not file security-impacting issues in the public tracker.
    </p>
    <p>
      Email{" "}<a href="mailto:office@bitcoin-austria.at">office@bitcoin-austria.at</a> with
      &ldquo;Kassiber security&rdquo; in the subject: what the issue is, the affected version or
      component, and reproduction steps if you have them. Never include descriptors, xpubs, seed
      words, or keys in a report. Please give us a reasonable chance to fix the issue before
      disclosing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In scope: the app, the CLI, this website, and the public nodes we run
      (<a href="https://mempool.bitcoin-austria.at">mempool.bitcoin-austria.at</a> and our Fulcrum
      instance). There is no Kassiber application server, so there is none to attack. Kassiber is
      maintained by a nonprofit, largely by volunteers: we will acknowledge and keep you informed,
      but there is no bug bounty and no guaranteed response time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="safe-harbor">
    <p class="section-number">02</p>
    <h2>Good-Faith Research</h2>
    <p>
      We support good-faith security research. If you make a genuine effort to avoid harm, respect
      other people's privacy and data, stay off systems that aren't yours or ours, skip
      denial-of-service and social engineering, and report what you find, we will treat your
      research as authorized and won't pursue legal action over accidental, good-faith violations.
      The code is AGPL and public; auditing it needs no permission at all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="model">
    <p class="section-number">03</p>
    <h2>What Kassiber Protects</h2>
    <p>
      The authoritative version, including every outbound request the app can make, is
      {" "}<a href={SECURITY_MD_URL}>SECURITY.md</a>. In outline:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No honeypot.</strong> Your addresses, balances, and identity are not aggregated on
        any server. Nothing to breach, subpoena, or sell.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Watch-only.</strong> No spending keys, ever. Nothing to coerce out of you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>At-rest encryption.</strong> Optional SQLCipher 4, keyed by a passphrase only in
        your head, so a stolen or seized device yields an unreadable database.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No telemetry.</strong> Update checks are opt-in, identifier-free, and never install
        anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Scoped AI.</strong> Local model by default, redacted read-only tools, per-call
        consent for anything that mutates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recoverable backups.</strong> <code>tar | age</code> archives restorable with stock
        tools even if this project disappears tomorrow.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="gaps">
    <p class="section-number">04</p>
    <h2>What It Does Not Protect</h2>
    <p>Stated plainly, because a security page that only lists strengths is marketing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A remote backend sees your addresses.</strong> The biggest privacy leak in normal
        use. The fix is your own node, optionally over Tor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not everything is inside the encryption boundary.</strong> Attachments, exports, and
        some config sit outside SQLCipher; pair it with full-disk encryption.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing protects an unlocked, compromised machine.</strong> Malware or root access
        defeats everything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Releases are currently unsigned.</strong> Check the SHA-256 manifest until release
        signing lands.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pre-alpha, unaudited.</strong> Expect bugs, including security-relevant ones.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="responsibilities">
    <p class="section-number">05</p>
    <h2>Your Part</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Run your own node where privacy matters;</li>
      <li>Use full-disk encryption and a passphrase you will actually remember;</li>
      <li>Verify downloads, keep software updated, keep offline backups;</li>
      <li>Keep AI prompts local unless you've consciously chosen otherwise.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <p class="section-number">06</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Security reports and questions:{" "}<a href="mailto:office@bitcoin-austria.at"
        >office@bitcoin-austria.at</a
      >. Ordinary bugs go to the{" "}<a href={`${GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL}/issues`}>issue tracker</a>;
      privacy requests are covered by the{" "}<a href="/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a>. When
      this page and{" "}<a href={SECURITY_MD_URL}>SECURITY.md</a> disagree, SECURITY.md is correct:
      it ships with the code.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
